Q: I want to render a 3x3 grid of boxes for a Tic-Tac-Toe game. I am wondering how I can utilize a .map() function if the box holds no information A: <h1>Issues</h1>
<p>The main issue with the code you have currently is that there is only a single <code>boxMark</code> state (<em>either <code>&quot;X&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;O&quot;</code></em>) that you are using for all 9 box buttons. You should have some state for each button individually.</p>
<p>The <code>boxes</code> array variable is not React state, it's redeclared as the empty &quot;sparse&quot; array each render cycle. <em>This</em> is what you need to store in state and update when buttons are clicked.</p>
<h1>Solution Suggestions</h1>
<ul>
<li>Move the boxes array into React state</li>
<li>Update the <code>handleMove</code> callback to
<ol>
<li>Update the appropriate boxes array element with the current player token</li>
<li>Toggle the player token</li>
</ol>
</li>
<li>Use the mapped boxes element value as each button's display value</li>
</ul>
<p>Notice as well once again functional state updates are used in order to compute the <em>next</em> state values from the <em>current</em> state values.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const { useState } = React;

export function Board() {
  // Start with player &quot;X&quot;.
  const [currentPlayer, setCurrentPlayer] = useState(&quot;X&quot;);

  // Initialize to array of length 9 filled with undefined values.
  // Really just about anything but &quot;X&quot; or &quot;O&quot; that is filled in during play
  // can work here, just use a value that makes sense and is easy to work with
  // in understanding what boxes are available. Undefined is a good choice since
  // it can be rendered directly and be ignored by React/JSX.
  const [boxes, setBoxes] = useState(() =&gt; Array(9).fill(undefined));

  function handleMove(index) {
    setBoxes(
      // Copy the boxes array, and for the element matched by index set
      // to the current player value.
      boxes =&gt; boxes.map((el, i) =&gt; i === index ? currentPlayer : el)

      // Or if your system supports Array.with (see note 1) which copies the
      // array and replaces with value at the specified index.
      // boxes =&gt; boxes.with(index, currentPlayer);
    );

    // Toggle the current player value.
    // If it's currently &quot;X&quot;, then return &quot;O&quot;, and vice-versa
    setCurrentPlayer(player =&gt; player === &quot;X&quot; ? &quot;O&quot; : &quot;X&quot;);
  }

  return (
    &lt;div&gt;
      &lt;h1 style={{textAlign: &quot;center&quot;}}&gt;Tic-Tac-Toe&lt;/h1&gt;
      &lt;h2 style={{textAlign: &quot;center&quot;, fontWeight: &quot;normal&quot;}}&gt;
        Next Player: {currentPlayer}
      &lt;/h2&gt;

      &lt;div className=&quot;grid-container&quot;&gt;
        {boxes.map((item, index) =&gt; (
          &lt;button
            key={index}
            className=&quot;square&quot;
            // Anonymous function, pass current mapped index to callback.
            onClick={() =&gt; handleMove(index)}
          &gt;
            {/* Use current mapped element value. */}
            {item}
          &lt;/button&gt;
        ))}
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p><sub><sup>1</sup> <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/with" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/with</a></sub></p>
